A bilatr fit can use many cores, in two ways: several chains at once,
and several threads within each chain. How many of each, and how the
work within a chain is split, decides how long a large panel takes to
fit. The best settings depend on the size of your data and on your
hardware, so this vignette shows how to measure them rather than
prescribing values.

## The four settings

* **`chains`** is the number of independent Markov chains. It is a
  statistical choice, not a speed one: R-hat needs several chains (4 is
  usual), and more chains give more draws.
* **`parallel_chains`** is how many of those chains run at the same
  time, each in its own process. With `chains = 4, parallel_chains = 4`
  all four run at once; with `parallel_chains = 1` they run one after
  another.
* **`threads_per_chain`** is the number of threads each chain uses for
  the likelihood. The models evaluate the likelihood with Stan's
  `reduce_sum`, which splits the dyads into tasks and runs the tasks in
  parallel, so a chain with 8 threads can work on 8 tasks at once.
* **`chunk_size`** is the `reduce_sum` grainsize: roughly how many dyads
  go into one task. It is stored as `C` in the Stan data. Small chunks
  mean many tasks, which share the work evenly across threads but cost
  scheduling overhead; large chunks mean little overhead but, with fewer
  tasks than threads, idle threads.

A fit uses about `parallel_chains * threads_per_chain` cores at its peak.

## Why the defaults are 1, 1 and 1

[fit_panel()] defaults to `threads_per_chain = 1`, `parallel_chains = 1`
and `chunk_size = 1`. These are conservative: they run on any machine,
including a laptop that is doing other work, a CI runner with two
cores, and a cluster job that was given one core, and they never
oversubscribe the CPU. They are also slow for a large panel. bilatr
cannot know how many cores you want it to use, so it does not guess;
choose the settings for your machine and data, using the steps below.

`chunk_size = 1` makes every dyad its own `reduce_sum` task. That is the
safest choice when nothing is known about the data, but it is rarely the
fastest, even with one thread: each task has a fixed cost, so measure a
larger value (see the sweep below).

## How they trade off

**Threads within a chain versus chains in parallel.** With 16 cores and
4 chains, you can run the 4 chains in parallel with 4 threads each, or
one at a time with 16 threads. Chains in parallel are nearly perfectly
efficient: four chains on four cores take about as long as one. Threads
within a chain are not: the parts of each iteration outside the
likelihood run on one thread, and the threads have to be coordinated.
So with cores to spare, first run the chains in parallel
(`parallel_chains = chains`), then give each chain the remaining cores
as threads. Threads matter most when you have more cores than chains,
or when one long chain is the bottleneck (for example, one chain per
cluster job).

**Chunk size versus scheduling overhead.** For a given number of
threads, throughput usually rises as `chunk_size` grows from 1 (less
overhead per task), levels off, and falls again once there are too few
tasks to keep every thread busy. The useful range scales with the
number of dyads: [benchmark_grid()] by default tries values from
`D / 200` to `D / 6`.

**Throughput versus cost.** More threads give more iterations per
second, but each extra thread adds less than the one before. Measured
in core-seconds per 1,000 iterations, the cost per draw rises with the
thread count. Which point to choose depends on whether wall time or
core time is scarcer: on a laptop it is wall time; on a shared cluster
with a core-hour budget, it may be core time. The Pareto front (the
settings for which no other setting is both faster and cheaper) is the
short list to choose from.

**Memory.** Each chain in parallel holds its own copy of the data and
its own output, so memory grows with `parallel_chains`; threads within
a chain share them. The model's memory is dominated by the latent
states, about `D * T` per draw. On a memory-limited machine, fewer
parallel chains with more threads each can fit where more parallel
chains cannot.

## Measuring on your own machine

[benchmark_bilatr()] runs a small sweep of `threads_per_chain` x
`chunk_size` on your data: one short chain per setting, timed. The
example below simulates a yearly panel of a few hundred directed dyads.

```{r simulate}
library(bilatr)
library(dplyr)

set.seed(2026)
countries <- sprintf("C%02d", 1:20)
years <- 2011:2020
classes <- c("0", "1", "2", "3", "4")
alpha <- c(0.3, 1, 1.2, -0.9, -1.6) # how each class responds to theta
mu <- c(0.2, 0.3, 0, 0, -0.3)

pairs <- expand.grid(actor1 = countries, actor2 = countries, stringsAsFactors = FALSE) %>%
  filter(actor1 != actor2)
panel <- purrr::pmap(pairs, function(actor1, actor2) {
  rate <- exp(runif(1, log(3), log(60))) # events per year
  theta <- cumsum(c(rnorm(1), rnorm(length(years) - 1, 0, 0.3)))
  counts <- t(vapply(theta, function(th) {
    p <- exp(alpha * th + mu)
    rmultinom(1, rpois(1, rate), p / sum(p))[, 1]
  }, numeric(length(classes))))
  colnames(counts) <- classes
  bind_cols(tibble::tibble(actor1 = actor1, actor2 = actor2, year = years), tibble::as_tibble(counts))
}) %>%
  bind_rows() %>%
  filter(rowSums(across(all_of(classes))) > 0)

n_distinct(panel$actor1, panel$actor2) # dyads
```

The machine this vignette was computed on:

```{r machine}
cpu <- if (Sys.info()[["sysname"]] == "Darwin") {
  system("sysctl -n machdep.cpu.brand_string", intern = TRUE)
} else {
  sub(".*: ", "", grep("model name", readLines("/proc/cpuinfo"), value = TRUE)[1])
}
cpu
parallel::detectCores()
R.version.string
cmdstanr::cmdstan_version()
```

The sweep: four thread counts, five chunk sizes, three replicates, each
run one chain of 50 warmup and 50 sampling iterations (with
`max_treedepth = 6`, so that every iteration of these short runs does a
similar amount of work; see `?benchmark_bilatr`). `...` passes
`time`, `action_cols` and `reference_category` on to
[prepare_stan_data()], as for [fit_panel()].

```{r sweep}
bench <- benchmark_bilatr(
  panel,
  threads_per_chain = c(1, 2, 4, 8),
  chunk_size = c(1, 5, 15, 40, 100),
  replicates = 3,
  iter_warmup = 50,
  iter_sampling = 50,
  time = "year",
  action_cols = all_of(classes),
  reference_category = "1"
)
bench
```

`sampling_sec` is the wall time of the sampling phase, `iter_per_sec`
the throughput and `core_sec_per_1k_iter` its cost in core-seconds.
`reduce_sum_wall_sec` is the time spent in the likelihood (warmup
included), from a copy of the model with the likelihood wrapped in a
Stan `profile()` block; the rest of each run is the single-threaded
part. `n_leapfrog` counts the gradient evaluations of the sampling
phase: the runs should all do about the same number, and
`benchmark_bilatr()` names any run that does not (its trajectory
drifted, so its `iter_per_sec` is not comparable). The medians over the
replicates below are robust to one such run.

[summarise_benchmark()] collapses the replicates, and
[benchmark_pareto()] marks the settings on the Pareto front of
throughput and cost:

```{r summarise}
summ <- bench %>%
  summarise_benchmark() %>%
  benchmark_pareto()

summ %>%
  select(threads_per_chain, chunk_size, iter_per_sec_median, core_sec_per_1k_iter_median, pareto) %>%
  arrange(desc(iter_per_sec_median))
```

[plot_benchmark()] shows the throughput over the grid, with the Pareto
front outlined (`autoplot(bench)` draws the same plot):

```{r heatmap, fig.cap = "Throughput (median iterations per second) over the grid; the Pareto front is outlined.", fig.alt = "Heatmap of sampling iterations per second over threads per chain (rows) and chunk size (columns), with Pareto-optimal cells outlined."}
plot_benchmark(summ)
```

and the trade-off itself is clearer as throughput against cost:

```{r pareto, fig.cap = "Throughput against cost, one point per setting; filled points are on the Pareto front.", fig.alt = "Throughput against cost per thousand iterations, one point per setting, colored by threads per chain; Pareto-optimal settings are labeled with their chunk size."}
library(ggplot2)
ggplot(summ, aes(core_sec_per_1k_iter_median, iter_per_sec_median, colour = factor(threads_per_chain))) +
  geom_point(aes(shape = pareto), size = 2.5) +
  geom_text(
    data = filter(summ, pareto),
    aes(label = paste0("chunk ", chunk_size)),
    nudge_y = 0.04 * diff(range(summ$iter_per_sec_median)), size = 3, show.legend = FALSE
  ) +
  scale_shape_manual(values = c(`FALSE` = 1, `TRUE` = 16)) +
  labs(
    x = "core-seconds per 1,000 iterations (cost)",
    y = "iterations per second (throughput)",
    colour = "threads_per_chain", shape = "Pareto front"
  ) +
  theme_minimal()
```

```{r best, include = FALSE}
fastest <- summ %>% slice_max(iter_per_sec_median, n = 1, with_ties = FALSE)
one_thread <- summ %>%
  filter(threads_per_chain == 1) %>%
  slice_max(iter_per_sec_median, n = 1, with_ties = FALSE)
speedup <- fastest$iter_per_sec_median / one_thread$iter_per_sec_median
default <- summ %>% filter(threads_per_chain == 1, chunk_size == 1)
```

On this machine (`r cpu`, `r parallel::detectCores()` cores) and this
panel, the fastest setting was `threads_per_chain = `r fastest$threads_per_chain``
with `chunk_size = `r fastest$chunk_size``, `r sprintf("%.1f", speedup)`
times the throughput of the best single-thread setting, at
`r sprintf("%.1f", fastest$core_sec_per_1k_iter_median / one_thread$core_sec_per_1k_iter_median)`
times the cost in core-seconds. Even on one thread, `chunk_size` mattered:
`chunk_size = `r one_thread$chunk_size`` ran
`r sprintf("%.1f", one_thread$iter_per_sec_median / default$iter_per_sec_median)`
times as fast as the default `chunk_size = 1`. Your numbers will differ: these runs are
short and timings are noisy, which is why the sweep has replicates.

To turn this into a fit with 4 chains, keep `chains = 4`, run as many
chains in parallel as your cores allow at the chosen thread count, and
pass the chosen `chunk_size`:

```{r, eval = FALSE}
fit <- fit_panel(
  panel,
  time = "year", action_cols = all_of(classes), reference_category = "1",
  chains = 4, parallel_chains = 2,
  threads_per_chain = 4, chunk_size = 15
)
```

## Measuring on a cluster

For a panel large enough to need a cluster, run the sweep there, with
one job per setting. The pieces:

1. [benchmark_grid()] defines the jobs. Its rows are ordered by thread
   count, so each thread count is one contiguous block, and one job
   array per thread count asks the scheduler for exactly that many
   cores.
2. [insert_reduce_sum_profile()] gives a copy of the model with the
   likelihood profiled. Write it to a `.stan` file and compile it once,
   before submitting.
3. Each job fits one chain and writes one row with the columns described
   in `?summarise_benchmark` ("Benchmark results").
4. Bind the rows and summarise them as above.

The preparation, on a login node:

```{r, eval = FALSE}
library(bilatr)

stan_data <- prepare_stan_data(panel, reference_category = "1")
saveRDS(stan_data, "bench/stan_data.rds")

grid <- benchmark_grid(stan_data$D, threads_per_chain = c(4, 8, 16, 32), replicates = 3)
readr::write_csv(grid, "bench/grid.csv")

src <- paste(readLines(system.file("stan", "bilatr_alphanorm.stan", package = "bilatr")), collapse = "\n")
writeLines(insert_reduce_sum_profile(src), "bench/bilatr_profiled.stan")
cmdstanr::cmdstan_model("bench/bilatr_profiled.stan", cpp_options = list(stan_threads = TRUE))
```

A worker script, `bench/worker.R`, run by each array task:

```{r, eval = FALSE}
library(bilatr)
threads <- as.integer(Sys.getenv("BENCH_THREADS"))
task <- as.integer(Sys.getenv("SLURM_ARRAY_TASK_ID"))

grid <- readr::read_csv("bench/grid.csv", show_col_types = FALSE)
job <- grid[grid$threads_per_chain == threads, ][task, ]

stan_data <- readRDS("bench/stan_data.rds")
stan_data$C <- job$chunk_size
mod <- cmdstanr::cmdstan_model("bench/bilatr_profiled.stan", cpp_options = list(stan_threads = TRUE))
fit <- mod$sample(
  data = stan_data, chains = 1, threads_per_chain = threads,
  iter_warmup = 100, iter_sampling = 100, seed = job$replicate, refresh = 0
)

sampling_sec <- fit$time()$chains$sampling
profiles <- fit$profiles()[[1]]
rs <- profiles[profiles$name == "reduce_sum_likelihood", ]
job$sampling_sec <- sampling_sec
job$iter_per_sec <- 100 / sampling_sec
job$core_sec_per_1k_iter <- threads * sampling_sec * 1000 / 100
job$reduce_sum_wall_sec <- sum(rs$total_time) / length(unique(rs$thread_id))
readr::write_csv(job, sprintf("bench/results/job_%04d.csv", job$job_id))
```

A SLURM array per thread count (here, the 8-thread block; with 9 chunk
sizes and 3 replicates it has 27 tasks):

```bash
#!/bin/bash
#SBATCH --job-name=bench_t08
#SBATCH --cpus-per-task=8
#SBATCH --array=1-27
#SBATCH --time=02:00:00
export BENCH_THREADS=8
Rscript bench/worker.R
```

When the arrays have finished:

```{r, eval = FALSE}
results <- purrr::map(list.files("bench/results", full.names = TRUE), readr::read_csv) %>%
  dplyr::bind_rows()
summ <- benchmark_pareto(summarise_benchmark(results))
plot_benchmark(summ)
```

If you benchmark several data sets at once, add a `scenario` column to
each job's row; [summarise_benchmark()] and [plot_benchmark()] group
and facet by it, and `benchmark_pareto(by = "scenario")` finds each
one's front.

## A worked result from a production panel

As an example of what a cluster sweep can show: for a yearly GDELT
panel of 16,750 directed dyads (the panel behind `?theta_directed`), a
sweep of this kind put `threads_per_chain = 16` with `chunk_size = 600`
on the Pareto front. That is about 28 chunks, a little under two tasks
per thread, a fairly coarse split, which suggests that on that cluster
the overhead of many small tasks outweighed the cost of uneven work.

This is an example, not a recommendation. The best settings depend on
the number of dyads and periods, the number of action types, the model,
the CPU and the cluster's scheduler; re-tune for your own data size and
hardware.

## Tuning the diagnostics

[diagnose_convergence()], [diagnose_and_extract_bilatr()] and
[extract_theta()] are the other place where throughput and memory
trade off, when they read a large fit from CmdStan CSV files:

* **`max_memory_mb`** is the memory budget for reading the per-period
  latent states (Tier 3) in chunks. Reading is single-threaded and each
  chunk costs about one pass over the largest CSV file, so fewer, larger
  chunks are faster: use the largest budget your job can afford. The
  default, 8 GB, is a guess, and the function says so when you rely on
  it.
* **`n_cores`** is the number of processes that compute R-hat and ESS
  for each chunk. It speeds up the summaries, but each process needs
  memory from the same budget, so at a fixed `max_memory_mb` more cores
  can mean more, smaller chunks and more passes over the files. Inside a
  SLURM job, `parallelly::availableCores()` gives the cores the job was
  allocated.

Both report their choice in a message before they start ("N variable(s)
in K chunk(s) ... estimated peak ..."); check K at your intended
`n_cores` before committing a long job to it. See
[`vignette("diagnostics")`](diagnostics.html) for the diagnostics themselves.
